<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grande Armée</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grande Armée was the main army of Napoleonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1812 it invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> with several hundred thousand men, but after the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and the winter retreat it was almost entirely destroyed, a turning point in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Grande Armée was formed in 1804 from the Armée des côtes de l'Océan, also called the Armée d'Angleterre, the field army of over 100,000 men that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had assembled at the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> for a planned invasion of the United Kingdom. When the Third Coalition formed against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after his coronation as Emperor of the French, the army turned east in 1805. At its formation it consisted of six corps led by Napoleon's marshals and senior generals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1805 rapid marches surrounded General Karl von Mack's Austrian army at Ulm, which yielded 60,000 prisoners for the loss of some 2,000 French soldiers. On 2 December 1805 the Grande Armée defeated a combined Russian and Austrian army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Battle of Austerlitz</a>, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> followed on 26 December. After the stalemate at Eylau, victory at the Battle of Friedland on 14 June 1807 brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaties of Tilsit</a>. The army was dissolved in October 1808 and its parts formed into the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><p>In 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> assembled the largest field army he had ever commanded to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and the battle of Borodino, the French reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 14 September 1812, but disease, desertion, exhaustion and long supply lines had already reduced the army. On the retreat, cold, starvation, disease and harassment by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> and partisans destroyed it as a fighting force, though its pontoon builders saved it from complete annihilation at the Berezina River. Later armies fought on in 1813, notably at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of the Nations</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, and the Hundred Days of 1815 ended its campaigns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The army was divided into corps, usually five to seven, each of between 10,000 and 50,000 men, commonly 20,000 to 30,000. Each corps was a self-contained army of combined arms, commanded by a marshal or a general, and the corps marched on separate routes within a day's march of one another. They were small enough to live by foraging, so fewer supplies had to be carried. The Imperial Guard formed a single corps of its own and served as a reserve.</p><p>Soldiers could rise through the ranks regardless of class, wealth or national origin. More than one-third of the troops who marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> were not French and came from 20 countries. Each soldier carried four days' provisions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> encouraged his men to live off the land. Every regiment included women called cantinières, married to soldiers, who sold food and drink and fed the troops when other arrangements broke down.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grande Armée” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Patriotic War of 1812, Napoleon's <b>Grande Armée</b> of around 600,000 men invaded Russia in June and was reduced to a small fraction of that number by December through Russian strategic withdrawal, the burning of Moscow, partisan warfare and the catastrophic winter retreat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Retreating from Moscow, Napoleon's <b>Grande Armée</b> began fighting its way across the Berezina River, in present-day Belarus, under attack by Russian forces under Wittgenstein and Chichagov.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Grande Armée</b> entered a largely deserted Moscow, and fires soon destroyed much of the city.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-14">14 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
